## Summary

In `hono/jsx`, the factory functions `jsx` / `jsxFn` (and therefore `createElement`, which is an alias of `jsx`) accept a narrower `children` type than what the `JSXNode` constructor and the `Child` type itself allow. This forces consumer libraries that call `createElement` directly with `JSXNode` or `Child` values to either cast or fall back to `hono/jsx/dom`'s `createElement`.

## Current declarations (`src/jsx/base.ts`)

```ts
export type Child = string | Promise<string> | number | JSXNode | null | undefined | boolean | Child[];

class JSXNode {
  constructor(tag: string | Function, props: Props, children: Child[]); // Child[] ✅
}

export const jsx: (
  tag: string | Function,
  props: Props | null,
  ...children: (string | number | HtmlEscapedString)[] // ❌ narrower than Child[]
) => JSXNode;

export const jsxFn: (
  tag: string | Function,
  props: Props,
  children: (string | number | HtmlEscapedString)[] // ❌ same
) => JSXNode;

export const cloneElement: (
  ...children: Child[] // ✅ correct
) => T;
```

Only `jsx` / `jsxFn` (and `createElement` via the alias) constrain children to a much smaller subset than `Child` allows. `JSXNode` constructor and `cloneElement` both accept `Child[]`.

